[Unit]
Description=Move NZBs between the arrs and TorBox (UsenetBlackhole client)
Documentation=file:/volume1/docker/arr-stack/scripts/usenet-blackhole.sh

# Both arrs are configured with a UsenetBlackhole download client instead of
# SABnzbd, because SABnzbd's server (nntp.torbox.app) serves articles only up
# to about 90 days old -- measured 2026-09-14 -- while TorBox's API fetched the
# same releases regardless of age. The arr writes an .nzb into its nzbFolder
# and polls its watchFolder; this is what moves the file between them.

[Service]
Type=oneshot

# TORBOX_API_KEY, MEDIA_ROOT. Read through lib/env-file.sh rather than exported.
EnvironmentFile=/volume1/docker/arr-stack/.env

# The log directory is created inside ExecStart, in the same shell that opens
# the redirect. An `ExecStartPre=/bin/mkdir` plus StandardOutput=append:<dir>
# cannot work: systemd applies the unit's StandardOutput to every process it
# spawns, ExecStartPre included, so mkdir dies with "Failed to set up standard
# output: No such file or directory" before it can create the directory the
# redirect points into. Observed as status=209/STDOUT on queue-cleanup.service.
#
# `exec` so the script is the process systemd tracks and its exit status is the
# unit's. `>>` rather than systemd's redirect, for the same reason.
#
# --report-failures is on here, and this file is where that decision lives. The
# script ships it off and takes a flag, which is right for a rollout but wrong
# as a resting state: nothing in a pass's output says reporting is off unless
# you already know to look for the line, so a stack that lost the flag reads
# exactly like one with nothing to report. That is how the retry storm survived
# -- 2,963 missing episodes, the same dead releases grabbed seven times each,
# and both arr queues empty because a blackhole item is never in one.
#
# Verified live on 2026-09-17 before this line went in: the pass reports
# `failure reporting: ON (POST /api/v3/history/failed)`, resolves releases to
# grabs in both arrs, and the ledger fills with `Radarr:267:torbox`-shaped
# entries. Re-copying this unit is how it stays on; the installed copy under
# ~/.config/systemd/user/ is a plain copy, not a symlink, so the two can drift
# and nothing will say so.
#
# --max-inflight 6 is the resting state, for the same reason --report-failures
# is on above: the script ships the cap inert and takes a flag, which is right
# for a rollout and wrong for a stack that is supposed to stay up. TorBox holds
# ten concurrent usenet downloads and refuses the eleventh, so 6 sits below the
# limit rather than at it, and the module's own docstring records why 6 was the
# value worth trying. Measured 2026-09-18, with no ceiling: 46 jobs submitted in
# one hour, 60 incomplete, and the arrs blind to all of them because a blackhole
# item is never in an arr queue.
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'mkdir -p /volume1/docker/arr-stack/logs && exec /bin/bash /volume1/docker/arr-stack/scripts/usenet-blackhole.sh --apply --report-failures --max-inflight 6 >> /volume1/docker/arr-stack/logs/usenet-blackhole.log 2>&1'
